window.docContents[145].resolve({"/ISO-80000-9-___-Physical-chemistry-and-molecular-physics/#iso80000-part9":{"contents":"The seventh part specified in full is ISO 80000-9, Physical chemistry and molecular\nphysics. Every item — all of 9-1 … 9-49, sixty-two including every sub-suffixed item — is\ncatalogued: counting entities and the mole, the molar quantities, the concentrations and\nfractions, the chemical potential and the activity ladder, the reaction and equilibrium\nquantities, statistical mechanics, and the transport and electrochemical coefficients —\neach carrying its exact source as data. Only citation locators are recorded; no normative\ncontent from the licensed standard is reproduced. The defining mathematics of selected\nremarks is specified in the sibling module Part9.DefiningRelations.\n\nPhysical chemistry is the home of the mole, and so the sharpest test in the series of\nthe Finkelstein–Whitehead scale-spanning reduction (the Scale-spanning units chapter,\nrequirement R13): the mole is a human-selected dimensionless count of entities, N_A of\nthem, so Dim.amountOfSubstance = 1, and every quantity per mole drops the mole. One\nreduction reorganizes the whole part. Amount of substance joins the dimensionless family.\nMolar mass is a mass, molar volume a volume, the amount-of-substance concentration a\nnumber density, molality an inverse mass. The seven J/mol quantities collide on one\ndimension; the three J/(mol·K) quantities on another.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"27. ISO 80000-9 — Physical chemistry and molecular physics","id":"/ISO-80000-9-___-Physical-chemistry-and-molecular-physics/#iso80000-part9"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Synopsis--Errors-prevented":{"contents":"* Bearer erased: pair P's A–B separation used where pair Q's is meant (HCP.L4_OutOfTheLine#whose_distance).* Observer left implicit: with PhysLib's single vector type, the geometric, boost and origin-shift conversions all accept the same vector; with kinded vectors, each conversion accepts only the kind it is correct for (HCP.L4_OutOfTheLine#bare_frames, HCP.L4_OutOfTheLine#kinded_frames).* Point/vector confusion: a position added to a displacement, or a distance added to the length of a displacement (HCP.L4_OutOfTheLine#points_vectors_and_distances).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L4 — Out of the line\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.5.1.4. Errors prevented","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Synopsis--Errors-prevented"}});